WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.102
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:04.838
you know from shows like
"Big Little Lies"

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:05.905
and "Parks and Recreation,"

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.373
as well as the film
"Step Brothers."

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:09.609
He stars in the fantastic
series "Severance,"

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.411
which is streaming
on Apple TV+.

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:12.345
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.246
-So what is my job?

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:17.117
-Are you really asking me that?

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:18.717
-Yeah.

00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:21.254
What is it we actually do here?

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:26.792
-We serve Kier, you child!

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:31.330
And until you get that through
your mildewed little brain

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.432
and hit quota,

00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:37.904
MDR's hallway privileges
are hereby revoked.

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:40.306
So get your little ass
back to your desk

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.510
and stay there
until you're told to move!

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.144
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:47.246
our friend Adam Scott,
everybody!

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:49.415
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:56.756 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.756 --> 00:01:04.130 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:07.600
So happy to have you here.
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.802
-I've got a million questions
about "Severance,"

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.937
but I want to start --
you posted this photo yest--

00:01:11.937 --> 00:01:13.239
Father's Day.
Happy Father's Day.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.674
-Thank you very much.
-Father of two.

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:15.808
-Thank you.

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:17.209
-You've got a couple
of teenagers.

00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:18.711
-A couple teenagers.

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:21.181
-And you posted this photo, and
it looks like -- What is that?

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.748
Like a box with a towel over it?

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:25.851
-Yeah, it's a last-minute
Father's Day gift,

00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:29.555
as Father's Day gifts
tend to be,

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:32.958
with a towel thrown over it
rather than wrapping paper.

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:34.460
-Gotcha.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.762
So they obviously knew that
you would immediately clock

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.498
they put very little thought
into the wrapping.

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.968
-Absolutely.
And they don't care.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.003
Because Father's Day
is kind of a joke.

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:45.638
-It's kind of a joke.
-Right?

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.173
-Yeah.
-Like, Mother's Day

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.108
is, like, the whole country
shuts down for a week.

00:01:49.108 --> 00:01:50.042
-Right.
-Rightly so.

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.477
-Rightfully so, exactly.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:53.079
-And dads are just dads.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.447
-Yeah.
-So we're fine.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:55.815
-I basically --
When my wife said,

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.216
"What do you want
on Father's Day?"

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.118
And I'm like, "I want to do
what I do every Sunday,

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:00.687
but I want you to not be mad."

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:03.522
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah!
That's great. That's great.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:04.824
-Just, like, a day where, like,

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:07.260
I'm gonna do all the same stuff
but no side eye.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.594
-That's right. Oh, man.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:11.197
-What was under the towel?

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.467
-Spikeball, the game,
which we already have.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:15.768
-Okay.

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.903
-Which is fine.
-Yeah.

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:21.573
-My son did -- He coded me
a computer program

00:02:21.573 --> 00:02:23.009
that he made just for me.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:24.510
-Okay, so that,
obviously, that means --

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.646
I mean, I'm impressed
that a kid can do that.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:27.980
-Yeah. Impressive.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.416
And it was like -- He had me
sit down at his computer,

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.018
and it kind of asks like

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:35.921
three or four rudimentary
kind of simple questions.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:39.192
But after a few questions, no
matter what answers I typed in,

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:42.228
it always just said,
"Adam is a penis."

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.164
[ Laughter ]

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:46.733
Which, frankly
made me even prouder.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.268
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:50.403
It makes me less proud of him
as a coder,

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:52.438
but the sense of humor,
it's like --

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.207
Yeah, the two are kind of
meeting in the middle.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:55.541
-Absolutely.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:58.310
-Your daughter, does she
also enjoy teasing you,

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.678
giving you a hard time?
-She does.

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:02.048
She's always trying to find
new ways to insult me.

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:03.983
The other day, I went into --

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:05.718
I was walking through
the living room,

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.854
and she was in there
on her laptop.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.723
And I was just like, "Hey,
honey, what are you up to?"

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:13.893
And she's like, "Nothing."

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:17.196
So I was like, "Okay, well,
I have to look at your computer

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.198
and see what you're looking at."

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:24.337
And no joke, she had just
googled "sick burns for actors."

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:26.839
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.512
And you know what?
It wasn't even Father's Day.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.480
-Yeah, that's very sweet
that she was thinking

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.582
about you on non-Father's Day.
-That's right.

00:03:36.582 --> 00:03:39.152
-So, we've spoken
in the past about --

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.054
And obviously they were younger.

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.222
It's been a while
since we talked about this,

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:45.258
but they did not enjoy
watching "Parks and Rec."

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:46.892
They did not like seeing you
in that,

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:48.360
even though
that is a show that --

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.730
I have heard from friends
of mine, kids love that show.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.298
They love that
it's about friends

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:54.233
and it has
a very family atmosphere.

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.102
But they did not ever
come around.

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:57.770
-Never watched it.
-Never watched it.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.672
-There was something about me
being in it

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:03.108
that ruined it for them
preemptively.

00:04:03.108 --> 00:04:04.310
They love "The Office"

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.878
and all the other shows
sort of around --

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.414
-In that universe, right.
But now, "Severance."

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.349
-Yeah.
-Are they watching it?

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:12.818
-Yeah, and they love it.
-That's great.

00:04:12.818 --> 00:04:15.821
What do you think is
the difference that tipped it

00:04:15.821 --> 00:04:18.357
towards being able to suffer
through their dad on screen?

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:20.759
-I don't know, because I feel
like I am on the verge

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:24.764
of ruining it for them
by being present inside of it.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.332
But I don't know.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.168
Something about the kind of
mystery puzzle box of it.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.337
And their friends are into it,

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:34.606
which I think is
part of why they like it.

00:04:34.606 --> 00:04:38.878
But I love that
they actually watch it.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:41.781
-Do you feel like you have to,
like, cloak a little bit

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.050
how psyched you are
that they're watching it?

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.785
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:47.853
-Because if you overdid it,
they would just tune right out.

00:04:47.853 --> 00:04:49.755
-Oh, my God.
If I burst into tears

00:04:49.755 --> 00:04:53.825
by the fact that they're
acknowledging my career,

00:04:53.825 --> 00:04:55.394
then they would
run away from it.

00:04:55.394 --> 00:04:56.963
-It also seems
a little dangerous

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.664
that their friends
like it, as well,

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.232
because you could not
just blow it for them,

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:01.601
but for their entire
social group.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:05.070
-I know. I even got a text
from one of my son's friends

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.440
just complimenting me
on the show,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.708
and I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:12.711
This is like -- Oh, my God.
This is a huge step."

00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:15.681
-That's really -- I just like
that he liked it so much

00:05:15.681 --> 00:05:17.983
that he said to your son,
"Can I get your dad's number?"

00:05:17.983 --> 00:05:20.920
-Yeah. I know. I know, I know.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.655
I had bragging rights
for a couple weeks.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.257
-That's allowed. That's only
allowed to work that way.

00:05:25.257 --> 00:05:26.526
-That's right.

00:05:26.526 --> 00:05:27.894
-You can't say like,
"Can I get your friends'..."

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:30.963
-Nope. Nope.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.499
That wouldn't go over well,
for some reason.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.501
-I actually -- So,
a friend of mine, he had a kid

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.969
in the Little League
World Series.

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:38.871
He hit, like,
a walk-off home run,

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:40.606
and I said to my friend -- This
is a high-school friend of mine.

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:43.175
-Yeah.
-And I'm like, "Oh, my God.

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:44.677
That's so great."

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:47.246
And he was like, "Could you
make a video for the team?"

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.947
I'm like, "sure."
He goes, "Great.
I'll give you his number."

00:05:48.947 --> 00:05:51.050
I'm like,
"I'm not sending it to him."

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.284
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:52.284 --> 00:05:54.387
-"I'll send it to you,
and then you show him."

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:56.022
-Needs to be a go-between.
-Yeah.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.759
-"Texting Videos to Teens
with Seth," yeah.

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.028
-Anything that starts with
"I can explain!"

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:05.398
-Yeah, yeah.
"Don't worry about it."

00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:06.566
-"It's about baseball."

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.333
Alright.
I have a ton of questions

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.636
about "Severance,"
which we're going to get to.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.705
We'll be right back with more
from Adam Scott.

